┃┓┃┃╻┃┣╸ ┃┗┫┏━┛  ┃┣┻┓╺━╸  ┃┣╸ ┃┏┛
┗┻┛┗┻┛┗━╸╹ ╹┗━╸  ╹┗━┛   ┗━┛┗━╸┗┛

ask Qwen3.8-27B a multiple-choice question and get the most likely answer, without generating any text.

    llm.decide(state, "did the deployment succeed?", ["yes", "no", "unclear"]).argmax
    -> "yes"

the model scores every possible next token anyway. we just take the best of A, B, C instead of sampling one and writing it out.

    $ make bench

    method                                     accuracy   seconds
    --------------------------------------------------------------
    read the probabilities (jev_v1)              94.8%    0.49
    force one letter with a grammar (jev_v2)     94.8%    0.34
    let the model write freely (plain)           89.7%    0.37
    any of the above, thinking first            100.0%     3.5

reading the probabilities and forcing a letter always pick the same answer. letting the model think first fixes every mistake but is ten times slower.


References:

- OpenJev, the same idea on a 4B model: https://github.com/TheoLeeCJ/openjev
- Jev, the closed service it reproduces: https://typesafe.ai
- Qwen3.8-27B: https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B
